                    <h2>Building Planning and Drawing Lab</h2>
                    <p class="content-text">
                        The Building Planning and Drawing Lab helps students learn how to design and plan buildings practically and effectively. In this lab, students work on drawings, layouts, and models to understand how buildings should be arranged for good use, safety, and comfort. It also teaches how to use space effectively and choose suitable building materials. The lab supports the development of skills needed for planning strong, efficient, and well-designed structures. The lab is equipped with 20 dedicated, state-of-the-art computers equipped with specialised software, enabling students to work on modern building design and drafting tools.
                    </p>

                    <p class="content-text">List of Experiments</p>
                    <ul class="content-text">
                        <li>Introduction to the tools and commands of drafting software.</li>
                        <li>Working in layers, blocks, x-ref, drawing layout and print setup.</li>
                        <li>3D drafting and rendering</li>
                        <li>Planning and drafting of the elevation and cross-section of door and window</li>
                        <li>Planning and drafting of the plan and cross-section of the Dog-legged and open well staircase.</li>
                        <li>Planning, drawing and modeling of a residential building of 1 room set.</li>
                        <li>Planning, drawing and modeling of 3 3-room residential building with staircase.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>Surveying and Geomatics Lab</h2>
                    <p class="content-text">
                        The Surveying and Geomatics Lab focuses on techniques and instruments used to measure and map the Earth’s surface. It provides practical training in land surveying methods, including total station surveys, levelling, and GPS measurements. Students learn to collect accurate spatial data for engineering projects, land development, and construction activities.
                    </p>

                    <p class="content-text">List of Experiments</p>
                    <ul class="content-text">
                        <li>To measure bearings of a closed traverse by prismatic compass and to adjust the traverse by graphical method.</li>
                        <li>To find out reduced levels of given points using auto/dumpy level.</li>
                        <li>To study parts of a Vernier and electronic theodolite and the measurement of horizontal and vertical angles.</li>
                        <li>To measure the horizontal angle between two objects by the repetition/reiteration method.</li>
                        <li>To determine the height of a vertical structure (e.g. chimney/ water tank, etc.) using trigonometrical leveling by taking observations in a single vertical plane.</li>
                        <li>To set out a simple circular curve by Rankine’s method.</li>
                        <li>Demonstration and working on Electronic Total Station. Measurement of distances, horizontal & vertical angles, coordinates and area of a land parcel.</li>
                        <li>Demonstration and working with Mirror stereoscopes, Parallax bar and Aerial photographs.</li>
                        <li>Visual Interpretation of standard FCC (False colour composite).</li>
                        <li>Digitization of physical features on a map/image using GIS software.</li>
                        <li>Coordinates measurement using GPS.</li>
                        <li>Demonstrate working of a Drone for survey of given field.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>Fluid Mechanics Lab</h2>
                    <p class="content-text">
                        The Fluid Mechanics Lab helps students understand how fluids behave in motion and at rest. It includes experiments on measuring flow rates, pressure, viscosity, and forces exerted by fluids. Students use devices such as venturi meters, orifice meters, flow channels, and pumps to observe real-life fluid behaviour. The lab strengthens theoretical concepts by providing hands-on experience, which is essential for designing hydraulic structures, water supply systems, and other engineering applications.
                    </p>

                    <p class="content-text">List of Experiments</p>
                    <ul class="content-text">
                        <li>To verify the momentum equation using the experimental setup on the impact of jet.</li>
                        <li>To determine the coefficient of discharge of an orifice of a given shape. Also, to determine the coefficient of velocity and the coefficient of contraction of the orifice mouthpiece.</li>
                        <li>To calibrate an orifice meter and study the variation of the co-efficient of discharge with the Reynolds number.</li>
                        <li>To calibrate a Venturimeter and study the variation of the co-efficient of discharge with the Reynolds number.</li>
                        <li>To calibrate a bend meter and study the variation of the co-efficient of discharge with the Reynolds number.</li>
                        <li>Verification of Bernoulli’s Theorem</li>
                        <li>To study the transition from laminar to turbulent flow and to determine the lower critical Reynolds number.</li>
                        <li>To study the velocity distribution in a pipe and also to compute the discharge by integrating the velocity profile.</li>
                        <li>To study the variation of friction factor, ‘f’ for turbulent flow in commercial pipes.</li>
                        <li>To study the boundary layer velocity profile over a flat plate and to determine the boundary layer thickness.</li>
                        <li>To determine the meta-centric height of a given ship model.</li>
                        <li>To determine the head loss for a sudden enlargement, sudden contraction and losses in bend.</li>
                        <li>Flow visualization -ideal flow</li>
                        <li>To make studies in wind tunnel (aerofoil and circular cylinder).</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>CAD Lab</h2>
                    <p class="content-text">
                        The Computer-Aided Design (CAD) Laboratory is a vital part of the Civil Engineering Department, designed to equip students with essential skills in digital drafting, modelling, and design. This state-of-the-art facility provides a platform for students to transition from traditional manual drafting methods to modern, software-based techniques widely used in the construction and design industries. The lab features 20 dedicated, state-of-the-art computers equipped with specialised CAD software, allowing students to create precise digital building designs, layouts, and models efficiently. By integrating theoretical knowledge with practical applications, the CAD Lab plays a key role in shaping industry-ready civil engineering professionals.
                    </p>

                    <p class="content-text">List of Experiments</p>
                    <ul class="content-text">
                        <li>Students will be able to work on Geo 5 /PLAXIS/STAAD Pro /Etabs software.</li>
                        <li>Students will be able to work on QGIS software.</li>
                        <li>Students will be able to design and analyze cantilever, gravity wall, retaining wall using geotechnical engineering software/design and analysis of multistory building using structural analysis and design software.</li>
                        <li>Students will be able to geo reference a given map using QGIS softwares.</li>
                        <li>Students will be able to prepare maps using QGIS.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2>Quantity Estimation and Management Lab</h2>
                    <p class="content-text">
                        The Quantity Estimation and Management Lab focuses on teaching students how to calculate material quantities required for construction projects and estimate their overall cost. Students learn to interpret building drawings, prepare detailed estimates, and develop cost schedules while becoming familiar with standard measurement practices, rates, and budgeting techniques. The lab helps students understand the financial aspect of construction work and prepares them for practical tasks in project planning and management.
                    </p>

                    <p class="content-text">List of Experiments</p>
                    <ul class="content-text">
                        <li>Interpret and analyze DSR (Delhi Schedule of Rates), CPWD (Central Public Works Department) specifications.</li>
                        <li>Estimate and analyze quantities of various items of work of a building.</li>
                        <li>Develop and prepare a Bill of Quantities (BOQ) for a project.</li>
                        <li>Utilize and apply MS Project to manage a project.</li>
                        <li>Evaluate a complete set of tender documents of a building.</li>
                    </ul>

